{"metabox":"1","type":"annotation","subject":"site/examples/src-auth.rs.qual","issuer":"mailto:alex@kesling.co","issuer_type":"ai","created_at":"2026-10-01T20:18:11.953032Z","id":"38fdff26ed0d545782049ef482af43ed29a4355a6f46c8cd1b3c526e283ae7fd","body":{"detail":"The playground (site/js/playground.js) runs the real qualifier wasm binary over site/examples/*.qual. Their IDs are placeholders: `g1h2i3...` and `h1h2h3...` are not hex, none match their content, and `d1d2d3d4...f9f0a1a2` is used both for the lib/crypto concern (src-auth.rs.qual line 3) and the reply on src/parser.rs (src-parser.rs.qual line 4). Running 0.8.0 over these files, `qualifier threads` omits the parser reply entirely (it collides with the crypto concern), and `qualifier show src/parser.rs` prints the reply as a top-level record. Visitors trying `threads` or `--format json` see behavior that a real file never produces. src-auth.rs.qual also holds subjects lib/crypto, lib/http and bin/server, at odds with its per-file name.","kind":"concern","span":{"start":{"line":3},"end":{"line":3},"content_hash":"5aa137932f0e0b6e84f907db8bb29c4c586e85f7b870ed1f9c6404ea0e230e35"},"suggested_fix":"Regenerate both files with the real CLI (`qualifier record`/`reply`/`resolve` in a scratch repo with fixed QUALIFIER_ISSUER) so IDs are genuine BLAKE3 values, and rename src-auth.rs.qual or split its other subjects into their own files.","summary":"Playground example records use fake, non-hex, and duplicated IDs, so the demo misrenders threads","tags":["review","review:2026-10-01","session:claude-code:906ddd45-2dc1-4a64-9674-56eec33bd6f1"]}}
